{"format":1,"seq":45,"writes":[{"type":"entry","value":{"model":[{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm realizing the extensions ref could be pinned to the PR's branch or SHA, but since the workflow definition for issue_comment events is always pulled from the default branch, testing against a real thread without merging means I'd need another approach, like adding a workflow input.\n\nAnother option is a repo-level variable override, but that affects all threads, so a better path might be a per-thread override tied to a label, requiring an extra step to resolve the PR head SHA via the API since it's not directly in the event payload. I'm also considering whether workflow_dispatch could work instead, though it's unclear if pi-action supports that trigger, and I'm checking whether pi-action installs `github:` extensions via npm from git, which seems to be the case since Pit installs from tagged source -- meaning a commit SHA reference should work fine.\n\nI'm thinking through a label-based opt-in design: a step that checks if the thread is a same-repo PR carrying a specific label, and if so resolves the head SHA and pins the extension to that immutable SHA instead of the released tag, which still satisfies the requirement that pi edits a separate checkout from the pit release being used. I'm restricting this to same-repo PRs (so the App token/secrets aren't exposed to forks) and noting that labeling requires triage+ access, which keeps things safe -- and I'm realizing the current PR itself could serve as the test thread for this.\n\nI need to figure out how to detect the PR context across different trigger types (issue_comment, pull_request_review_comment, pull_request) and extract the PR number accordingly, then use a shell step with gh api to resolve the SHA. The bigger snag is that workflow changes only take effect once merged to the default branch, so testing without merging this PR means I'll need a separate small PR to main just for the workflow change itself.\n\nOne alternative is testing in a scratch repo pointing pi-action at this branch's SHA directly, avoiding any main change entirely — though the user probably wants the real thing tested here, so I should mention that as a secondary option.\n\nI want to check whether pi-action's extensions input actually supports a `github:cv/pit#<sha>` style reference, so let me look at the pi-action README quickly.\n\n","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_bdrk_011yo96oYzvV8Qv7k8DUAus7","name":"typescript","arguments":{"code":"// @pit: {\"label\": \"Check how pi-action v2.5.4 resolves the extensions input\"}\nasync ({ gh: { api } }) => {\n  const r = await api(\"repos/cv/pi-action/contents/action.yml?ref=27723deedf4b41e99924171402c657c8d4e9ff69\");\n  const j = JSON.parse(r.stdout || \"{}\");\n  const text = j.content ? atob(j.content.replace(/\\n/g, \"\")) : r.stderr;\n  const i = text.indexOf(\"extensions\");\n  return text.slice(Math.max(0, i - 200), i + 1200);\n}"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"nvidia","model":"aws/anthropic/bedrock-claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":1804,"cacheRead":44723,"cacheWrite":2423,"totalTokens":48952,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0,"totalFromProvider":false},"cacheWrite1h":0,"reasoning":1551},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1794909054022,"responseId":"msg_bdrk_q4n4uvc3vmz7hmgfyvdtyeupkogxtttyomlyxr3i7pnpxk6nibkq","rawStopReason":"tool_use","durationMs":24184,"thinkingLevel":"medium"}],"kind":"pi.assistant","id":23,"conversationId":1,"byTaskId":22}},{"type":"task","value":{"id":22,"conversationId":1,"kind":"pi.generation","version":2,"input":{},"background":false,"abortRequested":false,"state":{"status":"waiting","checkpoint":{"phase":"tools","assistant":23,"tools":[24],"pending":[]},"on":[24],"policy":"allSettled"},"startedAt":1794909053987}},{"type":"task","value":{"id":24,"conversationId":1,"kind":"pi.tool","version":3,"input":{"kind":"model","a